{"claim_id": "S17-01", "task_id": "S17-T1", "claim": "T1 SATISFIED. A rank-1 twist acts by A -> A + lambda*Id and changes the trace by 2*lambda, so tr(A + lambda*Id) = 0 has the unique solution lambda = -tr(A)/2 (char 0). Within the orbit of a fixed connection under rank-1 twists there is therefore exactly one trace-free member. This is the part of the operator's argument that is correct.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "tracefree.py", "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "exact; solved symbolically, one solution returned."}
{"claim_id": "S17-02", "task_id": "S17-T2", "claim": "T2 FALSIFIED, and this contradicts the operator's stated reasoning in review #13. Trace-freeness ALONE does not single out a representative of a projective class. Counterexample exhibited explicitly over C(z): L = d - 1/(2z). Then L tensor L = d - 1/z is TRIVIAL, with rational witness w = z satisfying 2f + w'/w = 0; while L itself is NON-TRIVIAL, since the residue of f at 0 is -1/2, not an integer, so f is not the logarithmic derivative of a rational function. Tensoring a trace-free module by such a 2-torsion L preserves BOTH the projective class and triviality of the determinant. Hence a projective class can contain more than one trace-free module up to isomorphism, and 'exactly one trace-free representative per projective class' is false as a general statement.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "tracefree.py", "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "explicit counterexample; the triviality test is itself self-checked in both directions before use."}
{"claim_id": "S17-03", "task_id": "S17-METHOD", "claim": "DEFECT CAUGHT IN THIS SESSION'S OWN FIRST RUN: the T2 triviality check printed 'L tensor L trivial? False', which CONTRADICTED the conclusion drawn in the same block. The test compared 2f against -v'/v with v = 1/u instead of testing whether 2f is a logarithmic derivative at all; the correct witness is w = z, not 1/z. Replaced by a residue-based test (all poles simple, all residues integers, witness constructed and verified), which is self-checked on d - 1/z (trivial) and d - 1/(2z) (non-trivial) before being used. The conclusion was unchanged, but the evidence as first printed did not support it.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "tracefree.py", "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "recorded because a printed line contradicting its own conclusion is exactly the kind of thing that must not be left standing."}
{"claim_id": "S17-04", "task_id": "S17-T3", "claim": "T3 SATISFIED, and it is the decisive result. A second-order operator is determined by the PAIR (P,Q) in d^2 + P d + Q; the projective invariant R = P^2/4 + P'/2 - Q that CT-S16 matched is blind to a rank-1 twist, whereas (P,Q) is not. Comparing BOTH coefficients: System C in the z coordinate has P_C = (2z-1)/(z(z-1)) and Q_C = (22z^2 - 22z - 2 i sqrt(11) z + 5 + i sqrt(11))/(54 z(z-1)); the family's scalar operator at the located point (p,q,t,theta_inf) with cyclic vector e_1 has EXACTLY the same P and Q. P_F - P_C = 0 and Q_F - Q_C = 0 identically, with worst relative residual 0 over five points at dps 60. System C's differential module and the family's module at that point are therefore ISOMORPHIC, not merely projectively isomorphic.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": 60, "reproducible": true, "script": "tracefree.py", "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "both vacuity controls fired: a q perturbed by 1e-3 is rejected, and an operator with the same R but different P is separated by the (P,Q) test though not by R."}
{"claim_id": "S17-05", "task_id": "S17-T3", "claim": "STRUCTURAL REASON T3 holds, which is why it is not a coincidence: System C's leading coefficient factors as 3x^2 + x + 1 = 3(x - s_+)(x - s_-) = 3 delta^2 z(z-1) = 3 delta^2 D under x = s_+ + delta z. Hence System C's own Wronskian coefficient is P_C = delta(6x+1)/(3x^2+x+1) = D'/D, and the family's is P = D'/D - b'/b, which equals D'/D exactly when b is constant - i.e. when b_1 = 0, which is the condition the CT-S16 match already forced. The two objects carry the SAME rank-1 twist, so no twist has to be inserted between them.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "tracefree.py", "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "read off from the exact expressions printed by the run."}
{"claim_id": "S17-06", "task_id": "S17-CONSEQUENCE", "claim": "CONSEQUENCE FOR THE CT-S16 CAVEAT. The 'up to rank-1 twist' caveat is WITHDRAWN, but NOT for the reason given in review #13. The operator's conclusion is right and the operator's argument is wrong: uniqueness does not follow from trace-freeness (S17-02). It follows here from direct verification that the full operators agree (S17-04). The defensible wording is therefore: 'the differential module of System C is the module of the van der Put-Saito family at the located point of U_0', with no twist qualifier. Per the adopted P0 rule, a correct finding does not certify the reasoning around it; each adopted statement is verified on its own - and that rule is applied here to the operator's own review.", "label": "STRUCTURAL", "evidence_type": "proof", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": "tracefree.py", "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "adoption of the operator's conclusion, with its stated ground replaced."}
{"claim_id": "S17-07", "task_id": "S17-OPEN", "claim": "OPEN QUESTION, deliberately NOT asserted. Since trace-freeness alone is insufficient (S17-02), the correct sharper hypothesis under which a projective class has a unique representative in van der Put-Saito's normalised form is not settled here. A 2-torsion twist shifts local exponents by 1/2, but individual local exponents depend on the choice of lattice, so whether the normalisation 'local exponents +/- theta_i/2' excludes such twists needs an argument this session did not make. Reported as an open question rather than guessed, since the membership result (S17-04) does not depend on it.", "label": "CONJECTURED", "evidence_type": "proof", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": null, "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "flagged under the P0 halt rule on ambiguous conventions; no claim is made."}
{"claim_id": "S17-08", "task_id": "S17-MEMBERSHIP-LABEL", "claim": "The CT-S16 membership claim S16-14 is NOT raised above STRUCTURAL by this session, as pre-registered. The operator tied any such raise to step 3(b) - the monodromy and Stokes falsifier at 30 digits - which remains unrun (S16-15). What changes is the STATEMENT, not the label: membership is now about System C's actual differential module rather than its projectivisation.", "label": "STRUCTURAL", "evidence_type": "proof", "dps": null, "reproducible": true, "script": null, "output_hash": "ce471c5292edcea3f590c6338700c02f03dc0fe96ea178ecdec6eb2e1131336a", "review": "self", "review_note": "label discipline; fixed in PREREG.md before the computation was run."}
{"claim_id": "S17-09", "task_id": "adjudicate-CT-S18", "claim": "A rank-2 first-order system Y' = N Y admits TWO scalar reductions, the ODEs satisfied by its two components. Derived generically by elimination with no formula assumed: route 1 (for y1) has P = -tr N - n12'/n12, route 2 (for y2) has P = -tr N - n21'/n21. They differ by d/dz log(c/b) and are both correct. Neither is \"the\" scalar equation of the connection.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": 60, "reproducible": true, "script": "adjudicate_s18.py", "output_hash": "80303623301ce1cb3f404ee48ff13a05bc06bb134a589266163704c8439d6e8c"}
{"claim_id": "S17-10", "task_id": "adjudicate-CT-S18", "claim": "At the located point, route 1 reproduces System C exactly: P - P_C = 0 and Q - Q_C = 0 symbolically. Route 2 misses it by exactly -c'/c = -1/(z-q). db/dz = 0 and q not in {0,1} are re-confirmed at that point.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": 60, "reproducible": true, "script": "adjudicate_s18.py", "output_hash": "80303623301ce1cb3f404ee48ff13a05bc06bb134a589266163704c8439d6e8c"}
{"claim_id": "S17-11", "task_id": "adjudicate-CT-S18", "claim": "Control independent of both prior sessions: solving the SYSTEM at the ordinary point z0 = 3/10 + i/7 and testing which scalar ODE its y1 component satisfies gives route-1 residuals 4.8e-61 and 6.9e-62 against route-2 residuals 9.0e+00 and 4.0e+00, over two independent seeds. The control fires on both, so the test can distinguish the routes.", "label": "VERIFIED", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": 60, "reproducible": true, "script": "adjudicate_s18.py", "output_hash": "80303623301ce1cb3f404ee48ff13a05bc06bb134a589266163704c8439d6e8c"}
{"claim_id": "S17-12", "task_id": "adjudicate-CT-S18", "claim": "ADJUDICATION of CT-S18-COLDREAD-C3 claims S18-009, S18-010, S18-011: the computations are CORRECT but concern route 2, the scalar equation of the component eliminated using c(z). The inference that System C is therefore absent from chart U_0 is REJECTED. vdPS define q as the unique zero of c(z), so the route-2 scalar equation has an apparent singularity at z = q; System C has no third finite singular point and so cannot be route 2 for any point of the chart.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": 60, "reproducible": true, "script": "adjudicate_s18.py", "output_hash": "80303623301ce1cb3f404ee48ff13a05bc06bb134a589266163704c8439d6e8c"}
{"claim_id": "S17-13", "task_id": "adjudicate-CT-S18", "claim": "ADJUDICATION of CT-S18-COLDREAD-C3 claim S18-012 and its ERROR verdict on C3: REJECTED, as resting entirely on the route-2 comparison. CT-S18 tasks 1 and 2 are ADOPTED; task 1 independently reproduces the corrected b(z) including the same residual factorisation, so the C3 corrected b(z) support row moves from self to cold. The C3 moduli-space membership row does NOT move: a challenge raised and survived is not an independent confirmation.", "label": "STRUCTURAL", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": 60, "reproducible": true, "script": "adjudicate_s18.py", "output_hash": "80303623301ce1cb3f404ee48ff13a05bc06bb134a589266163704c8439d6e8c"}
{"claim_id": "S17-14", "task_id": "adjudicate-CT-S18", "claim": "Two method defects in CT-S18, recorded: (i) its P control was assert simplify(Pfam - Pfam) == 0, identically zero for any Pfam, so it could not fail and certified nothing, and it sat directly beneath the assertion driving the ERROR verdict; (ii) the mismatch was evaluated only at a generic sample (q=2/3, p=1/5, t=7/11) and never at the located point, and since the route-2 mismatch -1/(z-q) has no zero in z the test could only ever return mismatch. Neither defect touches task 1.", "label": "PROVEN", "evidence_type": "computation", "dps": 60, "reproducible": true, "script": "adjudicate_s18.py", "output_hash": "80303623301ce1cb3f404ee48ff13a05bc06bb134a589266163704c8439d6e8c"}
